{
  "id": "p4-w26-m1",
  "title": "Fewer bits = a coarser grid, and the outliers pay",
  "alt": "A hand-drawn whiteboard: four bit-strips drawn to scale for fp32, bf16, fp16 and int8 with the value 0.1 stored in each and its error written beside it; two number lines showing a weight snapping to the nearest representable value, tiny for fp16 and ten times larger for int4's sixteen levels; a weight-magnitude histogram whose narrow bell is followed by three circled outlier crosses far to the right labelled 0.1% of dimensions up to 20x larger; a crossed-out naive int8 box beside a ticked LLM.int8() box; and a Pareto scatter of GGUF quantizations from q2_K at 3.18 GB scoring 27 out of 50 up to q8_0 at 8.54 GB scoring 43, with the knee highlighted at q4_K_M.",
  "width": 1000,
  "height": 820,
  "tier": "T2",
